{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_bSl0D5ZscgAqdbt7bu6o7d6j","content":"1\t# Ossie interchange\n2\t\n3\t**Status: r2, draft. The owner ruled OD0–OD7 on 2026-11-10; the rulings\n4\tare in §10, and changes made for them are tagged `(o)`. Not reviewed**: the\n5\tselected instrument is the maintained light panel (OD0), and it has not run\n6\tover r1 or r2. This is not plan-gate approval: the plan gate follows the panel\n7\treview. `auto=false`. The plan approves no phase spec: OS0 needs its own owner\n8\tgate before any work, and no code is written before an OS1 spec is approved.\n9\t\n10\tPhase prefix `OS`. The owner asked on 2026-11-10 whether GND is compliant with\n11\tApache Ossie (incubating; formerly Open Semantic Interchange, OSI), what\n12\tcompliance would take, and to plan it. The owner added that PyRel is\n13\tOssie-compatible; §1.3 records what that turned out to mean.\n14\tInspection baseline: gnd-public main at `720053ff`; apache/ossie at\n15\t`ee89dbdc2844d8b7d293ed42c8613928bb99505f` and apache/ossie-converters at\n16\t`83ddc294c8840f5f497a2bdbec2348a128fc2254`, both read on 2026-11-10.\n17\t\n18\tGND writes its ORM 2 model as an Ossie **ontology document** (Ossie layer 4,\n19\tspec `ontology/ontology.md`, version `0.3.0.dev0`). That document passes\n20\tOssie's own validator and parser at a pinned commit. GND also reads such\n21\tdocuments back. Every GND construct that the Ossie ontology cannot express is\n22\texported losslessly in a GND vendor extension, or refused with a named\n23\treason. Nothing is dropped silently. Ossie's own PyRel converter is used as an\n24\tindependent consumer, to show that what GND writes is what a third party\n25\treads.\n26\t\n27\t## Completion predicates\n28\t\n29\tThe milestone is complete when, on main after merge:\n30\t\n31\t1. `gnd export ossie MODEL --out FILE` writes one Ossie ontology document for\n32\t   every packaged example model and every `gnd-model-from-text` example model\n33\t   (the \"export census\", fixed in OS0). Each document validates against the\n34\t   vendored `ontology/ontology.json` in-process (OS1-C1). It also passes\n35\t   upstream `validation/validate.py --schema ontology/ontology.json` and\n36\t   `OssieParser` at the pinned commits, in the opt-in `ossie_oracle` lane\n37\t   (OS1-C2).\n38\t2. Export is deterministic. The same model bytes give the same document bytes,\n39\t   and the document carries the model's identity digest (OS1-C3).\n40\t3. Every model element has exactly one disposition in a published,\n41\t   machine-readable loss report: `native` (a core Ossie construct), `requires`\n42\t   (an Ossie formula), `extension` (the `gnd` vendor extension) or `refused`\n43\t   (a closed reason code). A census test shows that no element of the export\n44\t   census lacks a disposition (OS1-C4, OS2-C4).\n45\t4. Constraints and derivations that the Ossie formula grammar can state are\n46\t   exported as `requires` and `derived_by` formulas that `OssieParser` accepts\n47\t   (OS2-C1). A population that violates a constraint shows the same verdict\n48\t   under GND's checker and under the formula's meaning. This is shown by\n49\t   differential cases, one per exported constraint kind (OS2-C2).\n50\t5. `gnd import ossie FILE --out MODEL` is total over arbitrary input. It\n51\t   refuses with exit 3 and a value-free `rule_id`, in one published order\n52\t   (OS3-C1, OS3-C2). For a GND-exported document, import restores a model that\n53\t   `gnd diff` reports as equal to the source (round trip, OS3-C3). A foreign\n54\t   document, such as Ossie's `examples/flights.ontology.yaml`, imports with\n55\t   `imported` provenance, or is refused element by element in the loss report\n56\t   (OS3-C4).\n57\t6. Ossie's RelationalAI converter (`ossie_relationalai`, pinned) converts\n58\t   every exported document of the census offline, with `declared_table` or\n59\t   with no mappings, in the opt-in lane. The concepts, properties, supertypes\n60\t   and identifiers in its PyRel output match the export's own declaration\n61\t   list (OS4-C1). Where the converter skips a construct, the skip is recorded\n62\t   in the loss report as a consumer limitation, not as GND's defect (OS4-C2).\n63\t7. An include-bearing model (model-composition MC1) is either exported as its\n64\t   composition or refused with `include:unsupported-route`. A test pins which\n65\t   (OS1-C5).\n66\t8. A docs page states exactly what is claimed (\"GND exports and imports Ossie\n67\t   0.3.0.dev0 ontology documents, validated at commit …\") and what is not\n68\t   (layers 1–3, semantic models, mappings, the query interfaces). It presents\n69\t   PyRel as optional and links `what-is-pyrel.md`. It names the deliberate\n70\t   divergences, such as `Duration` (o, OD5). The `gnd-export` skill cites the\n71\t   one normative home (OS5).\n72\t9. A contribution that makes `ossie_relationalai` emit `require` for Ossie\n73\t   `requires` formulas has been opened upstream, with the owner's approval of\n74\t   its text, and its state is recorded (OS6; o, OD7). An upstream merge is\n75\t   not required.\n76\t\n77\t## 0. Binding design rules\n78\t\n79\tThe six standing rules in `.codex/skills/process-phase/references/project.md`\n80\tbind every phase. They bind here as follows:\n81\t\n82\t| Rule | Where it bites in this milestone |\n83\t|---|---|\n84\t| 1 Totality | `gnd import ossie` reads foreign YAML/JSON. The reader, the formula parser GND adds and the extension decoder are each total over arbitrary input, with hostile-input tests (OS3). Export reads only validated models, but its loss-report reader is total too. |\n85\t| 2 Cap bounds the algorithm | Import resolves relationship names (`Concept.rel`), `extends` chains, `identify_by` paths and formula ASTs, which can be quadratic or deep. OS3 ships time-bound tests over each layer it adds. |\n86\t| 3 Validation order | Import order: byte cap, then YAML events, then the vendored JSON schema, then Ossie name rules (unique concepts, declared references), then GND admission, then the extension digest. The order is pinned by a source-order assertion (OS3-C2). |\n87\t| 4 One walk | One disposition walk over every metaschema element kind decides `native`/`requires`/`extension`/`refused`. There are no per-kind copies. The census test (predicate 3) is its witness. |\n88\t| 5 One home | The GND↔Ossie mapping is normative in one new section of `gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md` (\"Ossie interchange\"). Docs and skills cite it. The vendored Ossie schemas are sync-pinned copies of upstream (one artifact under the rule's scope note), held identical by a digest test against the pinned commit. |\n89\t| 6 Measure | OS0 measures the export census, the converter's offline behaviour and the formula grammar's reach before OS1–OS4 are specified. Contested mapping questions (role order, OneToOne, modality) are settled by experiments on the fixtures. |\n90\t\n91\tMilestone-specific rules (proposed):\n92\t\n93\t- **R7 Never claim more than the pinned bytes.** Every compliance sentence\n94\t  names the Ossie spec version and the two pinned commits. A re-pin is its own\n95\t  owner-gated change.\n96\t- **R8 Lossless or loud.** The loss report is part of the output contract. An\n97\t  element without a disposition is an internal error (exit 70), not a quiet\n98\t  omission.\n99\t- **R9 Credential-free by default.** Ordinary gates use only the vendored\n100\t  schemas and GND's own code. Upstream `validate.py`, `OssieParser` and\n101\t  `ossie_relationalai` run only in the opt-in `ossie_oracle` lane, in their\n102\t  own environments. They are never GND runtime dependencies (CONTRIBUTING; the\n103\t  `relationalai` package is proprietary and its converter extra is opt-in\n104\t  upstream).\n105\t\n106\t## 1. Scope, sources and authority\n107\t\n108\t### 1.1 What Ossie is, as read on 2026-11-10\n109\t\n110\t- Four layers (`core-spec/spec_layers.md`, informational): 1 Model\n111\t  Definition (`core-spec/spec.md`: datasets, fields, relationships, metrics,\n112\t  SQL expressions), 2 Relational Query Interface, 3 Wide Table Query\n113\t  Interface, 4 Ontology (`ontology/ontology.md`). Each layer \"can be adopted\n114\t  independently\".\n115\t- Version: 0.1.1 (2025-12-11) is the only release, and it has no ontology.\n116\t  0.3.0.dev0 is unreleased and its schema is mutable (\"do not depend on this\n117\t  version in production\"). The ontology layer exists only in 0.3.0.dev0.\n118\t- Ossie defines no single \"compliant\" certification. What exists is\n119\t  `validation/validate.py`, with `ossie-schema.json`, `ontology/ontology.json`\n120\t  and `ontology/mapping.json`; `compliance/portability` (an M0–M3\n121\t  converter-fidelity ladder over layer 1); and `compliance/query` (layer 2\n122\t  query cases).\n123\t- The ontology layer is ORM-shaped: `EntityType` and `ValueType` concepts,\n124\t  built-in value types, `extends` (subtypes), `identify_by` (preferred\n125\t  reference), n-ary relationships grouped under the concept that plays the\n126\t  first role, `verbalizes` reading patterns, `multiplicity` (`ManyToOne`,\n127\t  `OneToOne` only), `requires` (constraint formulas) and `derived_by`\n128\t  (derivation formulas, including recursion), plus optional IRIs.\n129\t\n130\t### 1.2 Why layer 4 and not layer 1\n131\t\n132\tGND models are conceptual. They carry no tables, columns or SQL. Layer 1\n133\trequires `datasets[].source` (a physical table) and SQL expressions. It could\n134\tonly be produced by inventing bindings or adding a physical-binding concept to\n135\tGND, and that is a new modelling feature (OD3). Layers 2 and 3 are query\n136\tengines, outside GND's purpose. Layer 4 maps onto GND's metaschema element for\n137\telement (§2).\n138\t\n139\t### 1.3 What \"PyRel is Ossie-compatible\" means here (measured, not assumed)\n140\t\n141\t- apache/ossie-converters ships `relationalai/` (`ossie_relationalai`,\n142\t  authored by RelationalAI). It converts **Ossie → PyRel** only. There is no\n143\t  PyRel → Ossie converter. It emits ontology-style PyRel (`m.Concept`,\n144\t  `m.Property(\"{A} has {B}\")`, `identify_by`, `define` rules from mappings).\n145\t- In the pinned snapshot (`tests/snapshots/.../flights/flights.py`), the\n146\t  converter emits **no `require`**. The Ossie `requires` constraints of\n147\t  `flights.ontology.yaml` do not reach PyRel. `derived_by` with `OR` or\n148\t  `EXISTS` raises `NotImplementedError` (`formula.py`). It pins\n149\t  `relationalai==1.39.0`, while GND's PyRel export pins `1.30.1`.\n150\t- GND's own `gnd export pyrel` writes rule-checking programs\n151\t  (`m.Relationship(fields=…)` plus `require` per constraint), not\n152\t  ontology-style declarations.\n153\t\n154\tTherefore GND → PyRel plus PyRel ⇄ Ossie does **not** make GND\n155\tOssie-compliant transitively. PyRel helps in three ways, and each is used:\n156\t\n157\t1. **An independent consumer (OS4).** The converter is a third-party reader\n158\t   of GND's Ossie output: if it builds the same concepts and properties, the\n159\t   export means what GND says.\n160\t2. **A user path.** GND model → Ossie document → `ossie_relationalai` → a\n161\t   RelationalAI model on Snowflake, alongside GND's existing PyRel rule\n162\t   export. GND's rule export stays the path for constraints, because the\n163\t   converter drops them.\n164\t3. **A shared idiom.** The converter's PyRel mirrors ORM (concepts, readings,\n165\t   identify_by), which confirms the §2 mapping direction. GND's requirement IR\n166\t   in `src/gnd/export/pyrel_rules.py` (`requirements(doc)`, one `Requirement`\n167\t   per constraint, domain and check) is the reuse seam for rendering Ossie\n168\t   `requires` formulas in OS2.\n169\t\n170\t### 1.4 Sources\n171\t\n172\t| Source | Section | Used for |\n173\t|---|---|---|\n174\t| owner request, 2026-11-10 | this conversation | goal; rulings OD0–OD8 (§10) |\n175\t| `ontology/ontology.md` @ee89dbdc | Concepts, Relationships, Identifying relationships, Derivation expressions, Requires, Global identifiers, Mapping documents | the target contract |\n176\t| `ontology/ontology.json`, `validation/validate.py` @ee89dbdc | — | oracle and vendored schema |\n177\t| `core-spec/spec_layers.md` @ee89dbdc | Layers | layer choice |\n178\t| ossie-converters `ontology/` and `relationalai/` @83ddc294 | README, `formula.py`, `converter.py`, snapshots | formula grammar, consumer oracle |\n179\t| `src/gnd/schema/orm-meta.schema.json` @720053ff | `$defs` object_type, fact_type, role, constraint kinds, derivation_rule | source contract |\n180\t| `src/gnd/export/pyrel_rules.py` @720053ff | `requirements`, `_refuse_unsupported_model` | reuse seam, refusal precedent |\n181\t| `src/gnd/rai_import.py` @720053ff | bounded `rai-orm-from-schema` import | import precedent (caps, provenance) |\n182\t| model-composition plan r3 | predicate 5 | include route guard |\n183\t| AGENTS.md, CONTRIBUTING.md | PRs, credential-free development | protocol |\n184\t| owner memory: PyRel optional, checkpoints | — | docs wording, checkpoints |\n185\t\n186\t## 2. Contract proposal and product behaviour\n187\t\n188\t### 2.1 Element mapping (proposal; OS0 measures it, OS1 fixes it)\n189\t\n190\t| GND metaschema | Ossie ontology | Disposition |\n191\t|---|---|---|\n192\t| object type `kind: entity` | `concept`, `type: EntityType` | native |\n193\t| object type `kind: value`, `primitive` | `type: ValueType`, `extends: [<built-in>]` | native; `Duration` has no built-in, so it is kept as a GND value type: `extends: [Integer]`, with its meaning in the extension (o, OD5, a deliberate divergence) |\n194\t| `subtype_of` | `extends` | native |\n195\t| subtype derivation rule | concept `derived_by` | requires/extension (OS2) |\n196\t| `reference` (preferred identifier) | `identify_by` and an identifying relationship | native |\n197\t| `independent`, `storage`, `description`, `provenance` | `description`; the rest in the extension | native + extension |\n198\t| fact type, roles, `role_name` | relationship under its first-role concept; `roles[].concept`, `roles[].name` | native; roles reordered so the uniqueness fits `ManyToOne` (o, OD4), with the original GND order kept in the extension for a lossless import |\n199\t| `reading`, `inverse_reading` | `verbalizes` patterns `{Concept}` / `{Concept:role}` | native |\n200\t| simple internal uniqueness on n−1 roles | `multiplicity: ManyToOne` (last role determined), with role reordering | native if reorderable, else requires/extension |\n201\t| uniqueness on both roles of a binary | `OneToOne` | native |\n202\t| spanning, external uniqueness; mandatory; frequency; ring; subset; equality; exclusion; exclusive-or; value range; value comparison; cardinality; subtype partition; guarded | `requires` formulas where the grammar can state them | requires, else extension |\n203\t| alethic/deontic `modality`, `source`, `status`, `rationale`, `remedy` | none | extension |\n204\t| derivation rules and aggregates | `derived_by`; `COUNT[...]`-style aggregates | requires/extension (OS2) |\n205\t| objectification | none native (Ossie has no nesting) | extension, or refused (OS0 measures) |\n206\t| operations, dynamic rules, frames | none | extension (opaque) |\n207\t| `includes` | none (no cross-model references) | compose or refuse (predicate 7) |\n208\t| `reference_populations`, sample populations | none in the ontology; mappings are layer 1 | out of scope |\n209\t\n210\t### 2.2 The `gnd` vendor extension\n211\t\n212\t`custom_extensions` exists in the layer-1 spec. Whether it is admitted on\n213\tontology concepts and relationships at the pinned commit is unverified until\n214\tOS0 checks `ontology.json`. If it is not admitted, the fallback is a sidecar\n215\t`FILE.gnd.json` bound by digest to the document (OD2). Content: canonical JSON\n216\tof the source elements that are not native, keyed by element id, plus the\n217\tmodel identity digest. Import verifies the digest before using any of it\n218\t(rule 3).\n219\t\n220\t### 2.3 CLI and exits (proposal)\n221\t\n222\t- `gnd export ossie MODEL --out FILE [--loss-report FILE] [--format yaml|json]`\n223\t  exits 0 on success. It exits 3 for an invalid model or a refused construct\n224\t  under `--strict` (OD6), 66 for missing input, 11 for an existing output (`EXIT_COLLISION`, the\n225\t  code `write_new` collisions already use) and 70 for an internal error.\n226\t- `gnd import ossie FILE --out MODEL [--loss-report FILE]` exits 0 or 3, with\n227\t  `rule_id`s in one order, and 11 if the output already exists.\n228\t- No network, no upstream package import, at runtime.\n229\t\n230\t## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes\n231\t\n232\t| Artifact | Home |\n233\t|---|---|\n234\t| exporter | `src/gnd/export/ossie.py` (new; layer map entry in `tools/gnd_layers.json`) |\n235\t| importer | `src/gnd/ossie_import.py` (new; same pattern as `rai_import.py`) |\n236\t| vendored schemas | `src/gnd/schema/ossie/ontology.json` (+ `mapping.json` only if OD3), with `PINNED.json` naming commit and sha256 |\n237\t| loss-report schema | `src/gnd/ossie-loss-report.schema.json` |\n238\t| normative mapping | `src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md`, new section \"Ossie interchange\" |\n239\t| CLI | `src/gnd/commands/export.py`, the import command group; CLI goldens under `tests/fixtures/rf1-pins/goldens/` regenerate |\n240\t| oracle lane | `tests/test_ossie_external_oracle.py`, marker `ossie_oracle` (new; registered beside `pyrel_oracle` in `pyproject.toml`); `tools/ossie_oracle.py` builds pinned upstream environments outside the repository |\n241\t| docs | `docs/explanation/ossie.md` and a how-to |\n242\t| planning | `.gnd-planning/milestones/ossie-interchange/` |\n243\t\n244\tUpstream prerequisites: neither package is on PyPI (`apache-ossie-ontology`\n245\tand `apache-ossie-relationalai` return 404 on 2026-11-10), so the oracle lane\n246\tinstalls from git at the pinned commits. `ossie_relationalai` needs the\n247\tproprietary `relationalai==1.39.0` and a `raiconfig.yaml`. Its offline mode\n248\t(`declared_table`) is claimed upstream, and OS0 measures it.\n249\t\n250\tPhase dependencies: OS0 → OS1 → {OS2, OS3} → OS4 → {OS5, OS6} → close.\n251\tOS3 needs only OS1's contract. OS4 needs OS1, and uses OS2 output where\n252\tavailable. OS6 needs OS2 and OS4 (o, OD7).\n253\t\n254\t## 4. Phases\n255\t\n256\t### OS0 — Pin, measure and decide (no product code)\n257\t\n258\tDeliverables: pinned upstream commits and digests; an isolated oracle\n259\tenvironment (scratch, outside the repository); the export census, listed;\n260\tfor every census model, a hand-run element inventory against §2.1; the\n261\texperiments below; the mapping table promoted to measured; and a checkpoint\n262\tpage for the owner.\n263\t\n264\tExperiments, recorded before they run:\n265\t\n266\t- E1: does `ontology.json` admit `custom_extensions` on concepts and\n267\t  relationships? (decides OD2)\n268\t- E2: does the `OssieParser` formula grammar express each GND constraint kind?\n269\t  This uses one hand-written formula per kind, from a census model.\n270\t- E3: does `ossie_relationalai` convert `flights.ontology.yaml` and one\n271\t  hand-translated GND example offline on relationalai 1.39.0? On a separate\n272\t  Python environment from GND's 1.30.1 PyRel environment.\n273\t- E3b (o, OD8): the same on relationalai 2.0.1 (released 2026-11-06; 2.0.0 on\n274\t  2026-11-03), overriding the converter's `==1.39.0` pin. The result tells OS4\n275\t  and OS6 which version to target. It is an observation only: GND's own PyRel\n276\t  pin (1.30.1) does not change in this milestone.\n277\t- E4: role order — how many census fact types need reordering to state their\n278\t  uniqueness as `ManyToOne`, and does reordering change readings? (OD4 is\n279\t  ruled \"reorder\"; E4 counts the exceptions)\n280\t- E5: upstream validator and parser run over Ossie's own examples, as a\n281\t  liveness proof that the oracle scores both ends: one valid document and one\n282\t  deliberately broken one.\n283\t\n284\tCriteria: OS0-C1, every census element has a measured disposition; OS0-C2,\n285\teach of E1–E5 (and E3b) has a recorded result with its limits; OS0-C3, the\n286\trulings that depend on a measurement are settled: OD2 by E1, OD4's exceptions\n287\tby E4, and the target `relationalai` version by E3/E3b (o; r1 wording: \"OD1–OD7\n288\tcan be ruled on measured cost\"). Spec: [phase-OS0-spec.md](phase-OS0-spec.md).\n289\t\n290\t### OS1 — Export core: types, fact types, readings, identifiers\n291\t\n292\tCovers the `native` rows of §2.1, the extension for everything else, the loss\n293\treport, determinism, the include guard, vendored schema validation and the\n294\toracle lane. Criteria: OS1-C1 to C5 (predicates 1, 2, 3, 7).\n295\t\n296\t### OS2 — Constraints and derivations as formulas\n297\t\n298\tA formula renderer over `pyrel_rules.requirements` (reuse, not a second\n299\tconstraint walk; rule 4). Each formula is checked by `OssieParser` in the\n300\toracle lane, and by GND's own grammar check for the subset it emits in\n301\tordinary gates. Includes differential cases, one per kind. Criteria: OS2-C1 to\n302\tC4 (predicates 3, 4).\n303\t\n304\t### OS3 — Import\n305\t\n306\tA total reader, caps, validation order, `imported` provenance, an extension\n307\tround trip, and foreign documents (the Ossie flights ontology). Criteria:\n308\tOS3-C1 to C4 (predicate 5).\n309\t\n310\t### OS4 — PyRel consumer cross-check\n311\t\n312\tIn the oracle lane: export → `ossie_relationalai` → PyRel source, then a\n313\tstructural comparison with the export's declaration list. Converter skips\n314\t(`requires`, `OR` or `EXISTS` derivations) go into the loss report as\n315\tconsumer limitations. The lane shows, beside the converter's output, what\n316\tGND's `gnd export pyrel --rules` adds for the same model. Criteria: OS4-C1,\n317\tC2 (predicate 6). The converter's dropped `requires` are the input to OS6\n318\t(o, OD7).\n319\t\n320\t### OS6 — Upstream contribution: `requires` → `require` in `ossie_relationalai` (o, OD7)\n321\t\n322\tThe converter emits no `require` for Ossie `requires` formulas, so the\n323\tconstraints GND exports (OS2) are lost on the way to PyRel. OS6 prepares a\n324\tcontribution to apache/ossie-converters `relationalai/` that renders each\n325\tparsed `requires` formula as `m.where(...).require(...)`. It is modelled on\n326\tGND's own rendering in `pyrel_rules.py`, re-derived under Apache-2.0 for that\n327\trepository, not copied wholesale. Upstream tests and snapshots are extended,\n328\tstarting with `flights`.\n329\t\n330\t- Deliverable: a branch in a fork, with upstream tests passing locally at the\n331\t  pin and the target `relationalai` version chosen from E3/E3b; then an\n332\t  upstream PR, posted only after the owner approves its exact title and body.\n333\t- Criteria: OS6-C1, upstream `relationalai/` tests pass, with new snapshot\n334\t  cases that contain `require`. OS6-C2, for every OS2 constraint kind the\n335\t  converter now renders, GND's OS4 lane observes a `require` and the\n336\t  corresponding consumer-limitation row in the loss report goes away. OS6-C3,\n337\t  the upstream PR URL and its review state are recorded. Merge upstream is\n338\t  not a criterion: the Ossie maintainers decide it.\n339\t- Follows the upstream repository's CONTRIBUTING and licence-header rules\n340\t  (`dev/check-license`). No GND private detail and no downstream application\n341\t  names go in the upstream text.\n342\t- Depends on OS2 (formula shapes) and OS4 (the lane that measures the effect).\n343\t  It does not block the close: if the PR is still open at close, its state is\n344\t  recorded as a carryover.\n345\t\n346\t### OS5 — Docs, skill, claim wording\n347\t\n348\tThe explanation page, a how-to with a business-level telling (`/gnd` skill\n349\tprompts and visual results, no Python on the tutorial page), the\n350\t`gnd-export` skill section, and the README one-liner. The review copy goes to\n351\tthe owner early: a checkpoint after OS1 (OS5-draft), not only at the end.\n352\tCriteria: OS5-C1 (predicate 8).\n353\t\n354\t### Close\n355\t\n356\tMilestone close record. Review instrument: the maintained light panel (o,\n357\tOD0).\n358\t\n359\t### Obligation-to-phase map\n360\t\n361\t| Obligation | Source | Phase / gate |\n362\t|---|---|---|\n363\t| answer \"what would compliance take\" with a falsifiable claim | owner 2026-11-10 | predicates 1–8 |\n364\t| use PyRel where it helps | owner 2026-11-10 | §1.3, OS4 |\n365\t| totality, caps, order, one walk, one home, measure | project.md | §0, every spec |\n366\t| include-bearing models refused or supported | model-composition predicate 5 | OS1-C5 |\n367\t| credential-free ordinary gates | AGENTS.md, CONTRIBUTING | R9, oracle lane |\n368\t| PyRel optional in docs | owner feedback 2026-10-15 | OS5-C1 |\n369\t| checkpoints before completion | owner feedback 2026-10-14 | OS0 page, OS5-draft |\n370\t| CLI goldens updated for new subcommands | rf1-pins | OS1, OS3 |\n371\t| try relationalai 2.0 | owner 2026-11-10 (OD8) | OS0 E3b |\n372\t| contribute `requires` → `require` upstream | owner 2026-11-10 (OD7) | OS6 |\n373\t| keep `Duration`, documented divergence | owner 2026-11-10 (OD5) | OS1, OS5 |\n374\t\n375\t## 5. Documentation, skill and compatibility surfaces\n376\t\n377\t- New subcommands change `gnd export --help` and `gnd import --help` goldens\n378\t  (`tests/fixtures/rf1-pins/goldens/cli-help/*`, `cli/tree.json`). OS1 and OS3\n379\t  regenerate them deliberately.\n380\t- No change to existing exports, the model format or populations.\n381\t- The `gnd-export` skill gains an Ossie section. `gnd-orm-core` holds the\n382\t  normative mapping.\n383\t\n384\t## 6. Risks and controls\n385\t\n386\t| Risk | Control |\n387\t|---|---|\n388\t| The 0.3.0.dev0 ontology schema changes under us | R7 pins; a digest test on the vendored copy; re-pin as an owner-gated amendment |\n389\t| A \"compliant\" claim read as certification | OS5 claim wording names layer, version and commits; no certification is run (project.md opt-in gates) |\n390\t| Formula grammar too weak for ORM constraints | E2 measures it; extension fallback; the loss report makes loss visible |\n391\t| Proprietary `relationalai` enters GND dependencies | R9; oracle lane in its own environment; never imported by `src/gnd` |\n392\t| Converter's offline mode does not work | E3 and E3b; OS4 then records the converter as unrunnable and predicate 6 is amended (owner) |\n393\t| relationalai 2.0.x is days old and the converter pins 1.39.0 | E3b is an observation only; OS4 and OS6 target the version E3/E3b show working, recorded with its digest |\n394\t| Upstream PR (OS6) is rejected or stalls | not a close criterion; the state is recorded as a carryover; GND's own rule export still covers constraints |\n395\t| Role reordering silently changes readings | E4; the mapping keeps the GND reading text verbatim in the extension |\n396\t\n397\t## 7. Out of scope\n398\t\n399\t- Layer 1 semantic models, mapping documents and SQL expressions, unless OD3\n400\t  selects them (then a successor milestone or an amendment).\n401\t- Layers 2 and 3, and the `compliance/query` and `compliance/portability`\n402\t  suites (layer 1 and 2 instruments).\n403\t- Running anything on Snowflake or a RelationalAI engine.\n404\t- Upstream contributions other than OS6 (in particular, to the Ossie spec\n405\t  itself, for example a `Duration` built-in). r1 excluded all upstream\n406\t  contributions; OD7 (o) moved the converter contribution in scope.\n407\t- Re-pinning GND's own PyRel export (1.30.1) to relationalai 2.x.\n408\t- Certification or recertification.\n409\t\n410\t## 8. Execution, approval and review protocol\n411\t\n412\t`auto=false`. Each phase: spec, owner gate, implementation, focused tests\n413\t(`uv run pytest -q tests/<phase-tests>.py …`), one independent reviewer in a\n414\tdifferent tool for the diff, refactoring review, record\n415\t(`NNN-phase-OS<n>.md`). Work goes on a topic branch with a PR; merge only on\n416\tthe owner's explicit authorization. Plan review and close review use the\n417\tmaintained light panel (o, OD0), via\n418\t`uv run python tools/run_gnd_review_panel.py --light REQUEST --out DIR`.\n419\tEach run needs the owner's go-ahead.\n420\t\n421\t## 9. Per-phase verification and affected consumers\n422\t\n423\t| Phase | Ordinary gate | Opt-in lane | Affected consumers |\n424\t|---|---|---|---|\n425\t| OS0 | none (no code); evidence under `evidence/OS0/` | E1–E5 | none |\n426\t| OS1 | `tests/test_ossie_export.py`, CLI golden tests | upstream validator and parser | `gnd export` help |\n427\t| OS2 | `tests/test_ossie_formulas.py`, `tests/test_pyrel_rules.py` | parser | PyRel rule export (shared IR; must not change) |\n428\t| OS3 | `tests/test_ossie_import.py`, `tests/test_diff*.py` | — | `gnd import` help |\n429\t| OS4 | — | `tests/test_ossie_external_oracle.py` | none |\n430\t| OS5 | docs tests (`tests/test_plan_app_docs.py` pattern for PyRel wording) | — | site |\n431\t| OS6 | upstream `relationalai/` test suite in the fork (outside this repository) | OS4 lane re-run | loss-report consumer rows |\n432\t\n433\tExact test file names are proposals until each spec fixes them.\n434\t\n435\t## 10. Owner decisions and open questions\n436\t\n437\tRulings were given by the owner in the conversation on 2026-11-10 (`(o)`),\n438\tquoted. The prior question and readings are kept in each row.\n439\t\n440\t| ID | Question | Readings and cost | Ruling |\n441\t|---|---|---|---|\n442\t| OD0 | Plan-review instrument | maintained panel (the bar), or one Codex reviewer as in model-composition (MD0) | (o) \"the light\": the maintained **light** panel (`gnd-review-panel-light`, default effort high) reviews the plan and the close. Selecting it does not run it. A run needs its own go-ahead, because it spends provider budget |\n443\t| OD1 | Target layer | (a) ontology only (proposed); (b) ontology plus layer 1, which needs physical bindings (OD3) | (o) \"ontology layer only\": (a) |\n444\t| OD2 | Where non-native elements go | `custom_extensions` in the document if E1 admits it; otherwise a digest-bound sidecar. The difference is whether a third party sees GND detail | (o) \"ok\": as proposed. E1 decides between the two |\n445\t| OD3 | Physical bindings for layer 1 and mappings | not now (proposed); or a new binding concept in the model (a model-format change, its own milestone) | Follows from OD1, not ruled separately: not now |\n446\t| OD4 | Role order | reorder roles to state uniqueness natively (more native, readings rewritten) or keep GND order and put uniqueness in `requires` (fewer native multiplicities) | (o) \"if it is not too difficult do it\": reorder. This is expected to be cheap: `verbalizes` patterns can name roles in any order (`'{Person:child} is a child of {Person}'`, ontology.md §Derivation expressions), so a reading need not change when roles are reordered. E4 confirms this. If E4 finds a fact type that cannot be reordered without changing its reading, OS1 keeps GND order for that fact type, puts its uniqueness in `requires`, and the record counts them |\n447\t| OD5 | `Duration` and other primitives with no Ossie built-in | `extends: Integer` plus extension (proposed), or refuse | (o) \"keep it, we will have to be different to Ossie here\": keep `Duration` as a GND value type (`ValueType`, `extends: [Integer]`, whole days) with its GND meaning in the extension. This is a deliberate, documented divergence from Ossie's built-ins (OS5 states it), not a refusal |\n448\t| OD6 | Default on a refused construct | write the document with a loss report and exit 0 (proposed), or exit 3 unless `--allow-loss` | (o) \"why not\": read as accepting the proposal. Exit 0 with the loss report; `--strict` turns any `refused` disposition into exit 3. If the owner meant the other reading, this row is re-ruled before OS1's spec |\n449\t| OD7 | Contribute `requires` → `require` to `ossie_relationalai` upstream | outside (proposed); or a follow-up after OS4 | (o) \"yes\": in scope as phase OS6 (§4). Opening the upstream PR is outward-facing, so its exact text gets a separate owner go-ahead before it is posted |\n450\t| OD8 | Which `relationalai` versions OS0 tries | 1.39.0 only (the converter's pin) | (o) \"you can also try relationalai 2.0\": E3 runs on 1.39.0 and on 2.0.1 (E3b). OS0 may install both, proprietary, in scratch environments only. This also answers OS0's D1 |"}
